{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "moves.spike.tirewear_mount",
    "description": "PLAN.md step 7c, the CONFIGURATION half. This document holds no logic: the 49 `data_sources` of `fixtures/process-tirewear.esm`, the 49 metaparameters their `extent` binds, the 53 index sets those metaparameters size, and one top-level `models` entry that is a `{ref}` to `tirewear_logic.esm`. Every line of it is the inlined fixture's own, except the 49 `url_template`s, which gained one `../` because this file sits one directory deeper than `fixtures/`.\n\nWHAT IT MEASURES. PLAN.md 7.1 calls the per-fixture document `thin`. This one is not: it is the whole of the fixture's configuration, and the `index_sets` block in it is a verbatim restatement of what `tirewear_logic.esm` already declares. The restatement is load-bearing and cannot be deleted as duplication: the ingesting build reads `file.index_sets`, this document's own registry, and a mounted child's do not enter it (F46). Delete the block and `esm validate` still passes while the build fails with `aggregate range ... references index set ..., which is not declared in the document index_sets registry`.\n\n`bindings` DOES NOT REMOVE THE RESTATEMENT and neither does `index_set_rename`; both were run at this mount edge rather than reasoned about, and what they do and do not do is recorded in docs/esm-conventions.md.\n\nHOW IT IS CHECKED. By emitting its relation and diffing it against the inlined fixture's, which is the only verification this shape leaves (F45, F47): an asserting model beside the mount makes every assertion in the document error, and the mounted child's own tests do not run.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "moves",
      "onroad",
      "spike",
      "phase7",
      "run",
      "tirewear"
    ]
  },
